# =====================================================================
#  EOS Script — BASIC example: blink an LED
# =====================================================================
#
#  What you'll learn here:
#    - the three parts of every script (declare, program, data)
#    - how a pin is declared and why INIT/SAFE matter
#    - the forever LOOP and DELAY
#
#  An .eos script is plain text. One command per line, like assembly.
#  Anything after a '#' is a comment and is ignored.
#
#  It runs FOREVER, starting the moment the console powers on. There is
#  no "main() returns" — the LOOP just keeps going.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- Part 1: DECLARE what hardware you use -----------------------------

# TARGET tells EOS whether the HD addon is fitted.
#   NOHD -> all of EXP1..EXP8 are free to use
#   HD   -> EXP1..EXP3 are taken by the HD addon; use EXP4..EXP8
TARGET NOHD

# USES claims a pin and says what it is.
#   EXP7 is a plain digital output (an LED, a relay coil, etc).
#   GPIO_OUT and PWM MUST declare INIT and SAFE:
#     INIT = the value driven the instant the script starts
#     SAFE = the value driven if the script ever faults
#   EXP pins have no guaranteed pull, so you must say what "off" is.
#   Here both are 0 (LED off at startup, off on any fault).
USES EXP7 AS GPIO_OUT INIT 0 SAFE 0

# ---- Part 2: the PROGRAM ----------------------------------------------

# LOOP ... ENDLOOP with no count means "repeat forever".
LOOP
  SET EXP7 1        # drive the pin HIGH -> LED on
  DELAY 500         # wait 500 milliseconds (MS is the default unit)
  SET EXP7 0        # drive the pin LOW  -> LED off
  DELAY 500         # wait another 500 ms
ENDLOOP

# END marks where the forever-loop wraps back to the top.
# (If you leave END off, it wraps at the last line anyway — but be tidy.)
END

# ---- Part 3: DATA -----------------------------------------------------
# This simple script has no payloads (no LED frames, no I2C blocks),
# so there is no DATA section. You'll see one in the next example.
